(window.COLLECTIONS = window.COLLECTIONS || []).push(
{
  id: "swords", route: "sword", label: "Valyrian Steel", glyph: "&#9876;",
  sub: (n) => n + " named blades that cannot be replaced",
  intro: "Valyrian steel is a lost art. It was forged with spells and dragonfire in the Freehold, it is lighter and sharper than any steel a modern smith can make, it never needs an edge put back on it, and — the fact that turns out to matter most — it kills the Others' creatures as surely as dragonglass. No one alive knows how to make more. Every blade below is therefore an heirloom, a political object, and a finite resource in a war nobody expected to fight.",
  items: [
    { id: "ice", name: "Ice", sub: "The greatsword of House Stark",
      meta: [["House", "Stark of Winterfell"], ["Form", "Greatsword, taller than a man"]],
      blurb: "Four hundred years of Stark justice in a single dark, rippled blade.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Ice is a greatsword of the old kind, meant for two hands and for standing work rather than for the press of a battle line. Set on its point it stands taller than the man who carries it. The blade is broad — wider across than a big man’s palm — and it tapers so gradually that the eye reads it as a long slab of darkness rather than a triangle. The steel is smoke-dark, nearer black than grey, and rippled all along its length with faint waves that seem to lie under the surface rather than on it, the way a current shows through clear water. Tilt it and the ripples move. In torchlight they run red at the edges. In the grey daylight of a Winterfell morning they go the colour of wet slate and are almost invisible, which is somehow worse.",
        "For its size it is a light thing. Ordinary steel cut to those dimensions would be a bar of iron with a handle, exhausting to hold level for the length of a sentencing; Ice can be raised and held steady by a man of ordinary strength, and comes down with an authority that has nothing to do with the arm behind it. The grip is bound in soft old leather, worn dark and shiny in the places where generations of the same family have put their hands. The crossguard is plain. There is no jewel, no gold, no boasting anywhere on it — the Starks apparently decided a long time ago that the sword was statement enough. It takes no oil to keep the edge and it has never been to a grindstone; nothing a smith could do would improve it. Drawn slowly it makes a low sound like a held breath.",
        "That is the part of Valyrian steel that can be described. The rest cannot, because the making of it is gone. It was forged in the Freehold with dragonfire and with spells whose words died with the men who spoke them, and the Doom took both. No smith living can make a new blade. The best of them, in Qohor, can reheat an old one and beat it into a different shape — that is the whole of the surviving art, and even that is a closely held secret and hideously expensive. So every Valyrian sword in the world is a fixed quantity, subtracted from and never added to. Men who own one tend to think of it as the family rather than as a weapon, and men who do not own one tend to think about how they might.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The older Ice", paras: [
          "The Starks say there was an Ice before this Ice. The name is far older than the blade that carries it now — the Kings in the North are supposed to have had a sword of that name back in the days when they still called themselves kings, and the histories are not agreed on what it was made of or where it went. Some accounts make it Valyrian too. Others suggest a much plainer weapon that simply held the name until something better came along. The one thing everyone agrees on is that Ice is a title as much as an object, and that the North has always had one.",
          "The greatsword that stands in Winterfell now is about four hundred years old, which makes it young by the standards of this list and ancient by any other. Where the Starks got it is not recorded in any way that satisfies. A house that far north, that far from the free cities and that famously uninterested in trade did not simply buy it off a table in Braavos, and yet no Stark boast survives about taking it from anyone. The gap in the record is large enough to have swallowed a story.",
        ] },
        { h: "In the hands of House Stark", paras: [
          "Ice was not a battle sword. Nobody carried it into a charge; a blade that size is a poor thing on a horse and a liability in a shield wall. It was carried out for beheadings, because the Starks kept the old way: the man who passes the sentence swings the sword, and he does it himself, in front of everyone, with the blade the family has used for the same purpose for centuries. Everything the North thinks about justice is contained in that ritual. The lord does not get to order a killing and then look away from it. If he cannot stand to do it, the argument runs, perhaps it should not be done.",
          "So the sword lived a strange, ceremonial life. It hung in the great hall or lay in its case; it came out for oaths, for judgements, and for the occasional visit from a king. Eddard Stark cleaned it himself afterwards, with an oilcloth, though the steel needs no cleaning and takes no rust — a habit inherited rather than reasoned, which is a fair description of most of what the Starks do. His sons watched him do it. That was the point.",
        ] },
        { h: "Carried south", paras: [
          "When Eddard Stark rode south to serve as Hand of the King he took Ice with him, and that decision looks stranger the longer one considers it. The sword belongs in Winterfell. It is the instrument of a northern custom that means nothing at all in a southern court where headsmen are hired and lords sign warrants. Taking it to King’s Landing was either a man refusing to leave his office behind him or a man who did not intend to be away long. Both readings are available and neither is comfortable.",
          "In the capital the greatsword was simply a curiosity — an antique from a cold place, admired by knights who priced it and by a queen who noticed it. Valyrian steel is not anonymous. Every court in Westeros keeps a rough ledger of who has a blade and who does not, and a house with a sword and no army is regarded very differently from a house with a sword and thirty thousand men. Ice went south into a room full of people who had been counting.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["After Eddard Stark's execution, Tywin Lannister had Ice melted down and reforged into two smaller swords — a deliberate, contemptuous unmaking of a rival house's identity. That the ancestral blade of the Starks was destroyed to make party favours is one of the cruellest things done in a story with a great deal of competition.",
        "The work was done by a smith brought out of Volantis, one of the few men alive with any part of the old craft, and even he could only reheat and rework what already existed. Some of the steel was lost in the process; there was enough for two longswords and not a scrap more. Four hundred years of a family’s justice came out of the fire as a wedding present and a keepsake."] },

    { id: "oathkeeper", name: "Oathkeeper", sub: "Half of Ice",
      meta: [["Bearer", "Brienne of Tarth"], ["Given by", "Jaime Lannister"]],
      blurb: "One of the two blades reforged from Ice — and given away almost at once to keep a promise.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Oathkeeper is a longsword, single-handed but with grip enough for two fingers of the off hand, and it is not the colour Valyrian steel is supposed to be. The reforging left something in it: the dark ripples are shot through with red, so that the blade reads black in shadow and rust-coloured in the sun, and the waves run in tighter, more agitated bands than the ones in the greatsword it came from. Smiths will tell you this is a matter of heat and folding. Anybody who has held it tends to prefer the other explanation. The hilt is gold, worked as a lion’s head with red stones for eyes — a Lannister object made from a Stark one, which was very much the intention.",
        "It is light, quick, and pitiless with an edge, and it will go through mail without the jarring shock a good castle-forged sword gives you. Valyrian steel cannot be made any more; there is exactly as much of it in the world as there was the day the Freehold burned, and every blade that gets cut down into smaller blades is a small permanent loss. Oathkeeper is the second half of that arithmetic — a new sword that is not new at all, only a rearrangement of something older that no longer exists.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Given away at once", paras: [
          "Jaime Lannister was handed one of the two swords cut from the Stark greatsword and got rid of it within days. He gave it to Brienne of Tarth, with instructions to use it to find the daughters of the man it had been taken from and to keep them safe, and she named it Oathkeeper on the spot — a rebuke and a promise in one word, aimed squarely at the man handing it over. It is the single clearest sign in the books that the Kingslayer is trying, extremely late, to become something else.",
          "There is a practical dimension people tend to skip past. Brienne is a woman of no standing riding alone through a country full of broken men. Jaime also gave her armour, a horse, a squire and a letter with his brother’s seal on it, and the sword was the least useful of those things right up until the moment it was the only useful one. A Valyrian blade is also a passport: men who would happily rob a big woman in plate think twice about a big woman in plate carrying something worth a castle.",
        ] },
        { h: "What it is for", paras: [
          "Almost no sword in this collection was made for a stated purpose. Ice was for sentences, Blackfyre was for kings, most of the rest are simply what a family happens to own. Oathkeeper is the exception: it was given, named and carried for one specific job, and the whole of its short history is the pursuit of two girls across a ruined countryside by someone with no authority to do it and no reason to give up.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "widows-wail", name: "Widow's Wail", sub: "The other half of Ice",
      meta: [["Bearer", "Joffrey Baratheon, then Jaime Lannister"]],
      blurb: "The second sword cut from Ice, named by a boy king who had it for a day.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Widow’s Wail is the twin of Oathkeeper and came off the same anvil in the same week: a longsword of dark steel banded with red, rippled in tight restless waves, lighter in the hand than its size suggests and sharp in the way only Valyrian steel is sharp — not honed to an edge but simply possessing one, permanently, without being asked. The hilt is gold and shaped as a lion’s head with rubies set for eyes, matching its brother almost exactly. Only the size differs, very slightly, and only if the two are laid side by side.",
        "Both swords exist because there is no other way to get a Valyrian blade. New ones cannot be made — the craft went into the ground with Valyria and the handful of Qohorik smiths who claim any part of it can only rework what already exists. So a man who wants one either inherits it, buys it from a family who will almost never sell, takes it, or melts down someone else’s. That is the entire market, and it explains a great deal about the behaviour of everyone in this collection.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Named at a wedding", paras: [
          "Joffrey Baratheon received the sword as a wedding gift, unwrapped it in front of the hall, and named it Widow’s Wail — a joke about killing husbands, delivered with enormous satisfaction, in a room full of men whose husbands and sons he had already made widows of. He then used it to butcher a book that had been given to him by his uncle, hacking the thing apart on the table while the guests worked out what expression to wear. It is a remarkable piece of characterisation: the boy gets an irreplaceable object and immediately finds something to ruin with it.",
        ] },
        { h: "Back to the Lannisters", paras: [
          "The blade did not stay with him long. It returned to his uncle Jaime, who by then had one hand and could do little with a sword beyond wear it, and who seems to have found the thing an embarrassment rather than a prize. He carried it through the riverlands without drawing it in anger. A sword named as a boast by someone who never used it, worn afterwards by a man who no longer could, is about as neat a summary of the Lannister position in those years as an object can manage.",
          "There is a small irony in the name. It was chosen as a threat and reads, later, as a description — though not in the direction its owner intended.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Joffrey was dead before the wedding feast ended, poisoned in front of several hundred witnesses, and the sword he had owned for a matter of hours went back into his family’s armoury. He remains the only person in this collection to name a Valyrian blade and never once carry it out of a room."] },

    { id: "longclaw", name: "Longclaw", sub: "The bastard sword of the Mormonts",
      meta: [["House", "Mormont of Bear Island"], ["Bearer", "Jon Snow"], ["Pommel", "A white wolf, recarved from a bear"]],
      blurb: "Given to a man who was not a Mormont, by a Lord Commander who had decided he was worth it.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Longclaw is a bastard sword — a hand-and-a-half, long enough in the grip for the second hand when the work gets serious, short enough to be used from a horse or in a corridor. It is not a showpiece. The blade is a little over three feet of smoke-dark steel, rippled the whole way with the faint wavering bands that mark the Freehold’s work, and in the low red light of a fire at the Wall those ripples look like something moving under ice. The edge is fine to the point of unsettling. Jon Snow has cut himself on it simply putting a thumb where a thumb should not go.",
        "The weight is the thing everyone notices first. A castle-forged bastard sword of that length is a genuine two-handed commitment; Longclaw can be swung one-handed all afternoon by a man of no particular size, and it does not tire the wrist. It shears through mail rather than crushing it. The grip is bound in soft leather that has been rebound at least twice in living memory. The pommel was a bear’s head in pale stone for five hundred years, because the Mormonts are bears; it is a wolf now, with garnets for eyes, recut by a Wall craftsman on a Lord Commander’s orders. The join is not invisible. That is arguably the best thing about it.",
        "Valyrian steel cannot be replaced. The Freehold made it with dragonfire and sorcery and the Doom took the recipe with everything else; there are perhaps a couple of hundred blades left in the world and there will never be another. A house that owns one owns it forever, or loses it forever. That is what makes what the Mormonts did with Longclaw so unusual.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Five hundred years on Bear Island", paras: [
          "Longclaw had been in House Mormont for something on the order of five centuries — a long tenure for a poor house on a cold rock in the Bay of Ice, and one of the reasons the Mormonts are taken seriously despite having almost nothing else. Bear Island is small, timbered, and short of everything. Its lords fight in mail their grandfathers wore. The sword was the household’s single object of real value, and the family’s insistence on keeping it rather than selling it during any of the several centuries in which selling it would have solved every problem they had says as much about them as anything in the histories.",
        ] },
        { h: "Jorah’s disgrace", paras: [
          "Jeor Mormont’s son Jorah brought the family into disgrace — slavers, debts, a wife with expensive habits and a sentence he did not stay to hear — and fled across the narrow sea. He left in a hurry and he left without the sword. Accounts differ slightly on whether the Old Bear physically prevented it or whether Jorah simply had no time; either way, Longclaw did not go to Essos, and the Mormonts’ opinion of the man who nearly took it there was expressed by his father taking the black rather than share a hall with the story.",
          "So Jeor Mormont came to the Wall as an old man and brought the sword with him, and there it stayed: an heirloom belonging to a family whose heir was an outlaw, in the hands of a man sworn to hold no lands and father no children. On paper it should have gone back to Bear Island. Jeor kept it, and kept it for years, and appears to have spent those years deciding what to do with it.",
        ] },
        { h: "Given to a bastard", paras: [
          "The decision came after a bad night in the Lord Commander’s own chambers. A dead man walked into the tower, and Jon Snow got there first, and the thing that finally stopped it was fire — a lamp, a curtain, a burning room — because steel had done nothing at all. Jeor Mormont gave Longclaw to him afterwards, along with the observation that a Mormont sword should stay in Mormont hands, and that he had decided the boy would do.",
          "Then he had the pommel recut. The bear became a white wolf with red stones for eyes, and the sword stopped being a Mormont blade in any sense the heralds would recognise. It is an act of adoption performed in metal, done by a man who could not adopt anyone, for a boy who had spent his whole life being told what he was not. Jon has been notably careful with it ever since. He does not lend it, he does not display it, and he has offered it back at least once to people with a better claim.",
        ] },
        { h: "What the sword turns out to be for", paras: [
          "The Watch had spent centuries treating the old stories as nursery talk. Longclaw is one of the objects that turns them back into a supply problem. Valyrian steel does to the enemies beyond the Wall what ordinary iron does not — the shattering of a White Walker at Hardhome, at the end of a very bad day on a beach full of people trying to get into boats, is the moment the arithmetic changes for everyone watching. There are two known substances that work. One of them is buried in the ground in quantity. The other is a fixed number of heirlooms scattered across a continent, mostly in the hands of families with no intention of going north.",
          "That is the joke the story keeps making with this collection. The swords were kept for prestige for a thousand years by men who thought the wars that mattered were in the south.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "heartsbane", name: "Heartsbane", sub: "The greatsword of House Tarly",
      meta: [["House", "Tarly of Horn Hill"], ["Form", "Greatsword"]],
      blurb: "Five hundred years in one family — taken by the son his father called useless.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Heartsbane is a greatsword, though a shorter and more practical one than the monstrous ceremonial blades of the North — it is meant to be swung in a battle, by a man in plate, at other men in plate, and the Tarlys have swung it in a great many. The steel is dark and rippled with the usual smoky waves, and it has the peculiar Valyrian quality of looking wet. There is a wolf’s-tooth curve to the last hand’s-breadth of the edge. The grip is long and plainly bound; the crossguard is heavy, square and scarred, which is unusual, because the guard is ordinary steel and the ordinary steel is the only part of the weapon that can be damaged.",
        "It is astonishingly light for its length. A fat, unathletic boy can carry it at a walk for weeks without his shoulder giving out, which is not a hypothetical. Nothing needs doing to it. It has never been sharpened by any Tarly and it never will be, because there is no whetstone in Westeros that would improve it and no smith who could repair it if he ruined it. The making of Valyrian steel is lost — burned out of the world with the Freehold — and the handful of surviving masters in Qohor can only reheat and reshape what already exists, at prices that make lords hesitate. A house that has held such a blade for five hundred years has, in a real sense, held the only truly irreplaceable thing it owns.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Five hundred years at Horn Hill", paras: [
          "House Tarly have been the sword-hand of the Reach for as long as the Reach has had one — bannermen to the Tyrells, and by common agreement among people who study these things the best soldiers in that part of the world. Heartsbane has been theirs for roughly five centuries. Where it came from before that, nobody says. The Tarlys have not needed to explain it; the sword and the reputation have reinforced each other for so long that the two are difficult to separate, and a Tarly host arriving on a field with that blade at the front of it has historically changed how the other side thinks about its afternoon.",
          "Randyll Tarly, the head of the house in living memory, is the most feared field commander in the Seven Kingdoms and a genuinely unpleasant man. He carried Heartsbane in the last war and did the only real damage the rebels took in the field. He is precise, humourless, and entirely without the sentimentality that the sword’s history might lead you to expect from him.",
        ] },
        { h: "The son who would not do", paras: [
          "His heir was Samwell — soft, bookish, frightened of blood, and the exact opposite of everything Randyll had planned to hand the sword to. What followed was one of the ugliest pieces of parenting in a story crowded with competition. The boy was starved of comfort, dressed in mail and made to sleep in it, marched, drilled, humiliated in front of the household and finally taken hunting and told, in a level voice, that he could take the black or be found dead in the woods with an arrow through him. The family sword was named explicitly in that conversation. It was the point of it: Randyll would not have Heartsbane pass to a coward, and had a second son who suited better.",
          "So Sam went to the Wall and Horn Hill got the heir it wanted. That should have been the end of the Tarly blade’s involvement in anything interesting.",
        ] },
        { h: "Taken", paras: [
          "On the screen it does not end there. Sam goes home years later, on his way to Oldtown, eats a spectacularly awful dinner with his father, and leaves in the small hours with the greatsword under his arm — not stolen in any way he could argue in a court, simply taken off the wall on his way out by the son who was told he was unworthy of it. He carries it to Oldtown and then, eventually, north, which is the only place in Westeros where the thing was ever going to be genuinely needed.",
          "On the page the sword is still at Horn Hill and Sam is still at sea, and Randyll’s threat about the inheritance is still just a threat. Whether the two accounts converge is one of the smaller open questions in the story, though it is hard to think of a better use for a five-hundred-year-old dragonsteel greatsword than putting it in the hands of the one man in Westeros who read everything the maesters wrote about what the swords were actually for.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "dark-sister", name: "Dark Sister", sub: "Visenya's blade",
      meta: [["First bearer", "Visenya Targaryen"], ["Later", "Brynden Rivers, 'Bloodraven'"]],
      blurb: "A slender Valyrian longsword made for a woman's hand, and carried by the most dangerous Targaryens.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Dark Sister is a longsword, but a narrow one — slimmer in the blade and lighter in the hand than a fighting man’s sword of the same length, with a shorter grip made for a smaller palm. It was built for speed and for the point rather than for hacking through plate, and everything about the proportions says it was made for a specific person. The steel is dark with the usual rippled waves running through it, close-set and fine, and it has a cold look even in firelight; where other Valyrian blades flush red near a hearth, this one seems to keep its own colour. The pommel and guard are plain, dark and unornamented.",
        "The whole thing weighs very little. A woman of ordinary build can hold it extended for a long time without shaking, which is precisely the advantage it was designed to confer, and in the hands of somebody fast it is a nightmare to defend against — it is quick coming in and quicker leaving. The edge has needed nothing done to it in three hundred years.",
        "Because that is the nature of the material. Valyrian steel was made with dragonfire and with spells nobody has recovered, and when the Doom took the Freehold it took the craft entire; no living smith can forge a new blade, and the best that can be managed is a Qohorik master reheating an old one into a new shape at ruinous cost. Every sword is therefore an inheritance, and inheritances get fought over. Dark Sister has been passing between the hands of extremely dangerous people for three centuries without once, so far as the records show, being sold.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Visenya", paras: [
          "It was Visenya Targaryen’s. She was the eldest of the three, the sister who trained with a sword while the world was looking at the dragons, and she wore Dark Sister as a working weapon rather than an ornament — one of a very small number of highborn women in Westerosi history to be taken entirely seriously with a blade in her hand. She rode Vhagar in the Conquest and she was, by every account that survives, the most frightening of the siblings by a considerable margin.",
          "The best-known story about the sword involves her using it on her own brother. Aegon was walking a street in his half-built capital with guards around him when the crowd got too close and knives came out; Visenya had him behind her and the situation resolved before the guards had finished turning round. Her point afterwards, made by drawing the blade and opening a shallow cut on the king’s cheek before anyone could stop her, was that his protection was too slow and too many. The Kingsguard — seven men, sworn for life, chosen for skill rather than birth — comes directly out of that afternoon. It is the only case in this collection of a sword being used to found an institution.",
        ] },
        { h: "Down the Targaryen line", paras: [
          "After Visenya the blade stayed in the family, and it went, as a rule, to the ones with a talent for violence rather than to the eldest. That is the pattern in it: Dark Sister is not the king’s sword and never pretended to be. It went to princes, second sons and cousins — men who fought.",
          "Prince Daemon Targaryen carried it during the reign of his brother and into the war that followed. Whatever else can be said about Daemon, and a great deal can, he was probably the finest swordsman his family produced, and the pairing of the most gifted Targaryen fighter with the sword made for the most gifted Targaryen fighter is exactly the kind of thing that made the rest of the court nervous. That the blade found its way to him rather than to the man on the throne tells you how the family sorted itself.",
        ] },
        { h: "Bloodraven", paras: [
          "The last well-documented owner is Brynden Rivers, called Bloodraven — a royal bastard with a wine-coloured birthmark, one eye, a reputation for sorcery, a thousand eyes and one, and one of the strangest careers in Westerosi history. He was a great archer before he was anything else, so a slender fast blade suited him. He fought in the Blackfyre rebellions, served as Hand of the King, ran the realm’s spies with a thoroughness that people still speak of uneasily, and was eventually sent to the Wall for something he had done that could not be publicly forgiven and privately could not be regretted.",
          "He took Dark Sister with him. A man condemned to the Night’s Watch keeps nothing, but nobody appears to have tried to take a Valyrian sword off Brynden Rivers. He rose to Lord Commander in due course, and the blade went north with him on rangings — into the one part of the world where Valyrian steel is not an heirloom but a piece of equipment.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Bloodraven took Dark Sister beyond the Wall on a ranging he never returned from. Where a Valyrian blade ended up in the country of the creatures Valyrian steel kills is a question the story leaves hanging, and it is hard to believe that is an accident.",
        "What became of the man is stranger than what became of the sword, and is dealt with elsewhere. The blade itself has not been reported in any hand since. It is one of the two great missing swords, and of the two it is the one whose absence sits in exactly the wrong place on the map."] },

    { id: "blackfyre", name: "Blackfyre", sub: "The sword of the kings",
      meta: [["First bearer", "Aegon the Conqueror"], ["Later", "Daemon Blackfyre and his line"]],
      blurb: "The blade every Targaryen king carried, given away to a bastard and turned into a claim.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Blackfyre is a bastard sword, hand-and-a-half, long enough to be used from the saddle with one hand and from the ground with two. It is the heavier and broader of the two swords the Targaryens brought out of the east, where Dark Sister is the lighter and finer, and the two are so alike in the character of the steel that maesters have wondered aloud whether they came off the same anvil, made as a pair for the same house before there were any dragons in Westeros at all. Nothing proves it. The suggestion has never quite gone away.",
        "The blade is black — genuinely black, not the smoke-grey that passes for it in most Valyrian work — and the ripples in it are deep and slow, more like the grain of old wood than like water. In sunlight it does not gleam so much as smoulder. The pommel and guard were reworked more than once over the centuries as fashions changed, which tells you something about how the sword was used: as regalia, mostly, worn at court by men who had a dragon for the serious work.",
        "It is Valyrian steel and therefore cannot be replaced, repaired by any ordinary smith, or dulled. The craft died with the Freehold; the surviving Qohorik masters can only reshape what exists. A sword that cannot be made again and cannot be destroyed by use is unusually well suited to being an emblem of legitimacy, and that is exactly what happened to this one.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The king’s sword", paras: [
          "Aegon the Conqueror wore Blackfyre. So did nearly every Targaryen king who followed him, for a century and a half, in a straightforward and slightly boring way: the man on the throne had the sword, the sword meant the man on the throne, and nobody wasted a thought on it. It was worn at coronations and at trials. It hung on the wall in the Red Keep. Its actual use in anger was rare, because the family had a shorter and more decisive argument available to it whenever it liked.",
          "Along the way it acquired the quiet weight that objects get when nobody questions them. To see Blackfyre on a man’s hip was to know, without asking, who he was. That is a very useful property in a sword and a catastrophic one in a gift.",
        ] },
        { h: "The gift", paras: [
          "Aegon the Fourth — called the Unworthy by his own realm, which took some doing — spent his reign fathering bastards, legitimising them on his deathbed, and generally arranging matters so that the century after him would be spent bleeding. Some years before the end he gave Blackfyre to his favourite natural son, Daemon, rather than to his trueborn heir. Whether it was affection, malice, a whim, or a considered piece of sabotage has been argued ever since and there is no way to settle it.",
          "What matters is what the realm heard. A sword nobody had ever seen on anyone but a king went to a bastard, and the bastard took the sword’s name as his house name and became Daemon Blackfyre. His half-brother Daeron got the crown and the throne and every legal argument, and looked bookish and quiet next to a magnificent young warrior wearing the emblem of kingship, and half the lords of Westeros drew the conclusion they had been invited to draw. A piece of steel had been turned into a claim, which is a thing swords can do only when everyone has already agreed what they mean.",
        ] },
        { h: "The rebellions", paras: [
          "The first Blackfyre rising ended on the Redgrass Field, and the fighting there was some of the worst the realm has seen — a very large battle, decided by archery from a hill at exactly the wrong moment for the men in the middle of it. Four more risings followed over the next two generations, each smaller and stranger than the last, and by the end the name had become less a claim than a habit.",
          "The sword left the country. Aegor Rivers, called Bittersteel — another of the Unworthy’s bastards, and the most implacably committed man in the whole affair — carried Blackfyre across the narrow sea after the first defeat and founded the Golden Company in exile, an army of the dispossessed built around the proposition that the sword would come home one day. The blade went with the pretenders for generations, brought back into Westeros with one rising, taken out again after the next.",
        ] },
        { h: "Where it is now", paras: [
          "That is where the record stops being satisfying. Blackfyre’s last confirmed whereabouts are with the exiled line, and the exiled line’s last confirmed male member ran out somewhere in the free cities in circumstances the histories describe with suspicious confidence. Some maesters say the sword is simply lost — sold, hidden, dropped in a harbour, hanging unrecognised in a Volantene counting house. Others assume the Golden Company kept it, because a company built on a claim does not mislay the claim.",
          "It is worth noticing what an object like this is for. A sword that everyone in Westeros recognises, that legitimises the man holding it before he has said a word, does not stay lost forever in a story that is running out of thrones. Of the two great missing blades, Blackfyre is the one people are actually looking for.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Blackfyre went east across the narrow sea with Bittersteel after the Redgrass Field and stayed with the exiled line for generations. Its current whereabouts are one of the great open questions of the books — and a sword that legitimises a claimant is exactly the kind of thing that does not stay lost forever.",
        "Daemon Blackfyre himself fell on the Redgrass Field with his eldest sons, shot down at the height of the battle by an archer on a hill who had once called him brother. That archer was Brynden Rivers, who took Dark Sister up afterwards. The two Valyrian blades of the Conqueror ended that day in the hands of two of his bastard great-grandsons, on opposite sides."] },

    { id: "brightroar", name: "Brightroar", sub: "The lost sword of the Lannisters",
      meta: [["House", "Lannister of Casterly Rock"], ["Status", "Lost"]],
      blurb: "The blade the richest family in Westeros bought, and then lost so completely they have been shopping for a replacement ever since.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Brightroar was a longsword, and by the descriptions that survive it was a showy one: a lion’s head worked into the pommel, gold on the fittings, the dark rippled Valyrian blade set off against as much Lannister ornament as could be attached without spoiling the balance. The steel itself would have been the usual smoke-grey shot with waves, catching the light in bands, taking an edge nobody ever had to put back. Everything else about it was the buyer’s taste rather than the maker’s.",
        "Nobody alive has seen it. What is certain is what is certain of all such blades — that it could not be reproduced, that no smith in Westeros could have mended it, and that the making of Valyrian steel died in the Doom along with the men who knew the spells. It follows that a family who lost one had lost it permanently, and that no amount of gold could commission another. That last point is the whole of Brightroar’s significance, and House Lannister has never entirely got over it.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Bought, not won", paras: [
          "The Lannisters did not take Brightroar off anybody and no ancestor wrestled it from a dragon. They bought it, during the century of chaos after the Doom, when the surviving Valyrian families in the free cities were selling everything that was not nailed down and a great many things that were. The price is recorded only as a comparison: it is said to have cost as much as raising and provisioning an army.",
          "That is an unusually honest provenance for a Westerosi heirloom, and it is also very much in character. House Lannister’s power has always run on gold first and swords second, and the acquisition of a Valyrian blade by simple purchase — the only method available to a family with no ancient claim to one — is the Rock in miniature. It also gave every rival house a joke to make at their expense, which may explain some of what came next.",
        ] },
        { h: "Lost with the Valyria expedition", paras: [
          "King Tommen the Second of the Rock took Brightroar east. He fitted out a great fleet and sailed for the Smoking Sea to plunder the ruins of Valyria itself — a place that men had been sailing carefully around for a century, on the grounds that ships which go in do not come out. He was not deterred. Neither the king nor the fleet nor the sword was seen again.",
          "The Lannisters have never satisfactorily explained the decision. The kindest reading is that a young king wanted a legend; the more likely one is that Valyria’s ruins were widely believed to be stacked with dragon gold and unclaimed steel, and that a family who bought their first Valyrian blade fancied collecting the rest for free. Whatever the reasoning, the result is that the wealthiest house in Westeros put its single irreplaceable possession on a boat and sailed it into the one part of the world nobody returns from.",
        ] },
        { h: "The long shopping trip", paras: [
          "What followed has a faint air of embarrassment about it. Successive Lannisters made discreet enquiries, and later much less discreet ones, about buying a Valyrian blade from any house that would part with one. None would. The great families with swords understood exactly what was being asked and enjoyed refusing it; a house that sells its Valyrian steel to Casterly Rock is a house announcing that it is finished. Tywin Lannister is said to have made offers to more than one impoverished lord and to have been turned down every time, which for a man of his temperament will not have improved matters.",
          "There was also a second expedition, quieter than the first. Gerion Lannister — Tywin’s youngest brother, the funny one, the one everybody liked — took a ship east to look for the sword. He got as far as Volantis. Nothing certain has been heard of him since, which makes Brightroar the only object in this collection to have cost two members of the same family.",
          "Understanding all that makes a certain later Lannister decision look less like spite and more like appetite. A house that has spent two centuries failing to buy Valyrian steel, presented with a rival’s ancestral greatsword and no reason to give it back, was never going to hang it on a wall.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "lady-forlorn", name: "Lady Forlorn", sub: "The blade of House Corbray",
      meta: [["House", "Corbray of Heart's Home"], ["Region", "The Vale"]],
      blurb: "A Valyrian longsword with a red stone in the pommel, carried by the Vale's most feared duellist.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Lady Forlorn is a longsword of ordinary length and entirely ordinary silhouette, which is part of its reputation — from ten paces it looks like any knight’s sword until it is drawn. The blade is dark and rippled, and in the thin cold light of the Vale it reads almost blue. There is a single red stone set in the pommel, big enough to see across a hall, and it is the only decoration on the weapon. The grip is long enough for one hand and a little more. The whole thing is balanced very far back, which makes it fast in the recovery and is why the men who have carried it tend to be duellists rather than battlefield brawlers.",
        "It is light, it never dulls, and it will take a hand off through a mail gauntlet without noticeably slowing. As with every blade on this list, that quality cannot be reproduced: the Freehold’s smiths are ash, the spells went with them, and no forge in the world can do more than reheat and reshape steel that already exists. In the Vale, where the great houses are old and proud and short of ready money, the fact that House Corbray owns something no amount of Arryn or Royce gold could purchase counts for a very great deal at a council table.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The Corbrays of Heart’s Home", paras: [
          "House Corbray came over with the Andals, which in the Vale is worth saying out loud, and their sigil is three black ravens carrying red hearts in their claws — an image that is either heraldic poetry or a warning depending on the century. Lady Forlorn goes with the lordship. That is the rule at Heart’s Home: the blade passes to the head of the house, and the head of the house carries it, and the matter is not open for discussion.",
          "They have used it, too. Corbrays turn up in the Vale’s wars with a regularity that suggests the sword was rarely idle, and their reputation is for personal violence rather than for generalship — a family of dangerous individuals rather than a family of commanders. The blade suits that. It is not a sword for breaking a line; it is a sword for the twenty seconds after two men have found each other in a crowd.",
        ] },
        { h: "On the Trident", paras: [
          "The most celebrated recent use came in the last great rebellion, at the ford, in the general disaster of that afternoon. Ser Lyn Corbray put Lady Forlorn through Prince Lewyn of Dorne — a Kingsguard knight, one of the seven, and the man commanding the Dornish spears in the royal host. Killing a white cloak in open battle is the kind of thing a knight’s whole subsequent life gets measured against, and Ser Lyn has never been shy about the measuring.",
          "It made him famous and it made him dangerous, and it did not make him popular. The Vale’s opinion of Lyn Corbray is fairly consistent: brilliant with a sword, unpleasant to be near, and not a man anyone wants in the room during a negotiation.",
        ] },
        { h: "The awkward inheritance", paras: [
          "Here is where the family rule broke. Lyn is a younger son. His elder brother Lyonel holds Heart’s Home and the title, and the sword — by every custom the Corbrays have — should hang at Lyonel’s hip. It does not. Their father gave Lady Forlorn to Lyn on his deathbed, and the elder brother has been living with that ever since: a lord with a castle and no blade, and a landless younger brother with the family’s soul in his scabbard and no intention of handing it over.",
          "In the tangled, faintly poisonous politics of the Vale, this matters more than it sounds. A man with a Valyrian sword and a reputation for using it is a faction in his own right, whatever his acreage. Lyn Corbray has been used as an instrument by cleverer men, has agreed to be used, and has almost certainly been paid for it; when the lords declarant confronted the man they blamed for the Vale’s troubles, it was Lyn who put a hand on the hilt and turned a debate into something much closer to a killing. Whether that scene was genuine or staged for the benefit of the room is a question the Vale is still chewing on.",
          "The unresolved thing about Lady Forlorn is therefore not where it is — everyone knows where it is — but who it belongs to, and what the man carrying it will do next."] },
      ] },

    { id: "nightfall", name: "Nightfall", sub: "The sword of House Harlaw",
      meta: [["House", "Harlaw of Ten Towers"], ["Region", "The Iron Islands"]],
      blurb: "A Valyrian blade in the hands of the ironborn's most thoughtful house.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Nightfall is a longsword, dark-bladed and rippled in the usual way, with a crescent moon worked into the pommel in mother-of-pearl and moonstone — a pale ornament on a black weapon, and a very deliberate piece of design. Beyond that the descriptions are thin. It is light, it holds an edge nobody has ever had to restore, and it is one of a small handful of such blades anywhere in the islands.",
        "That scarcity is the whole story. Valyrian steel cannot be forged any more; the craft burned with the Freehold and no living smith can do more than reshape a blade that already exists. The Iron Islands are poor, rocky and proudly hostile to the idea of paying for anything, so a Valyrian sword there is a considerably rarer object than in the green lands to the east — rare enough that owning one is a permanent argument in your favour at a kingsmoot.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "House Harlaw of Ten Towers", paras: [
          "Nightfall belongs to House Harlaw, the largest and steadiest of the ironborn houses, whose lord — Rodrik Harlaw, called the Reader — is famous throughout the islands for the eccentric and slightly shameful habit of reading books. He has the finest library on the Iron Islands and is regarded by his own countrymen as a bit of an embarrassment for it, which is a fair summary of ironborn intellectual life.",
          "That the islands’ most bookish lord holds one of their few Valyrian swords is a nice irony, and one the ironborn largely fail to appreciate. Rodrik is not a coward and has fought when it was required of him; he simply thinks the reaving is a dead end and has said so, calmly, in rooms full of men who wanted to hit him for it. The sword does not, in the end, hang at his own hip — the Harlaws have kept it in the hands of a kinsman known for using it, which is the sort of arrangement a practical family makes.",
        ] },
        { h: "A short record", paras: [
          "There is very little else to tell, and it would be dishonest to pretend otherwise. Nightfall has no famous duel attached to it, no theft, no disappearance, no king who carried it into a ruin. It has simply been a Harlaw possession for a long time, on an archipelago whose histories are largely oral and largely boasting. What it does have is a future: the Iron Islands sit on the western sea, the ironborn have lately been taking an interest in matters far outside their usual reach, and a named Valyrian blade in the hands of an ambitious house rarely stays quiet for long.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "red-rain", name: "Red Rain", sub: "The Drumm blade",
      meta: [["House", "Drumm of Old Wyk"], ["Region", "The Iron Islands"]],
      blurb: "A Valyrian sword the ironborn say was taken the honest way — by reaving.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Red Rain is a longsword whose steel is said to hold a red cast — the dark rippled waves of Valyrian work shot through with something closer to rust or old wine than to the usual smoke-grey. Whether that is genuine or whether generations of Drumms have simply insisted on it to suit the name is not a question anyone in the Iron Islands is keen to test. In any light it is a dark sword. The fittings are plain. Nothing about it is pretty.",
        "Like every blade in this collection it is lighter than its size promises, sharper than any smith could make it, and permanently so; and like every blade in this collection it cannot be replaced, because Valyria is a poisoned ruin and the art went into the sea with it. On islands as poor as these, a weapon worth more than the castle it hangs in is not a curiosity but a political fact.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Taken, they say", paras: [
          "The Drumms hold that an ancestor — Hilmar Drumm, remembered as the Cunning — took Red Rain off an Andal knight by force during the long wars over the islands, and the version they prefer has him doing it while armed with almost nothing worth the name. The story exists to make a point about cleverness beating equipment, and the ironborn tell it constantly.",
          "Whether any of it happened is impossible to say. What matters is the claim itself: in the islands, a sword taken in a fight is worth more than a sword bought with coin, and the Drumms have spent centuries making sure everyone remembers which kind theirs is. It is the same instinct that makes the ironborn call payment cowardice and theft a religion. The name of the blade suggests, at minimum, that the previous owner did not hand it over willingly.",
        ] },
        { h: "Since then", paras: [
          "The honest position is that not much has happened to Red Rain since. It has stayed with House Drumm of Old Wyk, passing to the head of the house — currently the old and stubborn Dunstan Drumm — and being produced at gatherings where the ownership of an irreplaceable weapon does the arguing for its owner. When the islands last gathered to choose a king by acclaim, the sword was in the room and everyone knew it. That is chiefly what a Valyrian blade is for in the Iron Islands: not the killing, which they can manage with axes, but the standing.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "vigilance", name: "Vigilance", sub: "The Hightower sword",
      meta: [["House", "Hightower of Oldtown"], ["Region", "The Reach"]],
      blurb: "The ancestral Valyrian blade of the oldest and richest house in the Reach.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Vigilance is a longsword of the Hightowers, dark and rippled and unremarkable to look at beside the wealth that surrounds it. What is recorded of its appearance is little; what can be said with confidence is what can be said of all such blades — that it is far lighter than its length suggests, that its edge has never been ground and never will be, that it will part mail without complaint, and that it makes a low clean note when it comes out of the scabbard.",
        "The reason it matters is the reason all of them matter. Valyrian steel cannot be made. The Freehold’s smiths worked with dragonfire and with spells that were never written down for outsiders, and the Doom ended both in an afternoon; every existing blade is a survivor and no new ones are coming. For a house like the Hightowers, who could buy most things in Westeros twice over, owning one of the few objects gold cannot produce is a particular kind of satisfaction.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The house that does not need it", paras: [
          "House Hightower is older than the Andals, older than most of the Seven Kingdoms, and rules Oldtown from a lighthouse that has been burning since before anyone was counting. They have the Citadel in their city, the Faith’s heart was there for centuries, and their vaults are widely believed to rival Casterly Rock’s. All of which means the Hightowers have very rarely needed to swing anything. Their preferred instruments are the maesters, the septons, marriage and money, deployed patiently over generations, and it has worked so well that people routinely forget how powerful they are.",
          "Vigilance therefore spends most of its existence as a statement rather than a weapon: worn at ceremonies, mentioned in inventories, and quietly noted by everyone who does business in Oldtown.",
        ] },
        { h: "The one war it is remembered in", paras: [
          "The exception is the Dance of the Dragons. Lord Ormund Hightower brought the strength of the Reach out for the greens and carried Vigilance through that campaign, up through the west and north toward the capital, in what was for a while the most successful land army in the war. It is the only period in which the Hightower blade appears repeatedly in the histories doing what a sword does.",
          "Beyond that the record is thin, and there is no point inventing more. Vigilance has had a long, comfortable, largely uneventful life in one of the safest cities in Westeros — which, for a Valyrian sword, is a much better outcome than most of the blades on this list managed.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "lamentation", name: "Lamentation", sub: "The Royce blade",
      meta: [["House", "Royce of Runestone"], ["Region", "The Vale"]],
      blurb: "A Valyrian sword held by one of the oldest First Men houses in the Vale.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Lamentation is a Valyrian longsword and almost nothing about its appearance survives in any account worth trusting. What can be said is generic and true: dark steel with the drowned-water ripple running through it, a weight that surprises anyone who picks it up expecting a normal sword, an edge that has outlived every man who ever admired it, and a name that suggests its earlier owners were not sentimental people.",
        "It cannot be replaced. That sentence is the whole of Valyrian steel’s meaning in Westeros — the Freehold is a poisoned ruin, the craft is gone, and the number of blades in the world only ever goes down. It is why a sword like this ends up meaning more to a family than most of their land does.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "The bronze house", paras: [
          "House Royce of Runestone are First Men to the bone and have never let anyone forget it. They wear ancient bronze armour carved over with runes that nobody now living can properly read, they hold a seat older than the Andal invasion, and their pride is a byword even in the Vale, which is a region built out of pride. Yohn Royce — Bronze Yohn — is the head of the house in living memory and one of the few lords in the Vale whom everyone, including his enemies, takes entirely seriously.",
          "That such a house carries a sword out of Valyria is a small monument to how thoroughly the two cultures eventually mixed. The Royces resisted the Andals, lost, absorbed them, and ended up holding an Andal-era luxury alongside the runed bronze of the people who were there first. Nobody at Runestone appears to find this contradictory.",
        ] },
        { h: "Very little else", paras: [
          "It would be padding to pretend Lamentation has a story. It does not. There is no famous duel, no theft, no disappearance, no king who carried it into a ruin — only a very old family holding a very old sword in a mountainous corner of the realm that has spent most of the recent wars deliberately sitting them out. If the Vale ever commits its considerable strength to something, the blade will presumably be at the front of it. Until then, the most interesting thing about Lamentation is that it exists at all, in the hands of the one house in Westeros least likely to have bought it from anyone.",
        ] },
      ] },

    { id: "orphan-maker", name: "Orphan-Maker", sub: "The Roxton blade",
      meta: [["House", "Roxton of the Ring"], ["Region", "The Reach"]],
      blurb: "A Valyrian sword with an unusually honest name.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "Orphan-Maker is a Valyrian longsword, dark and rippled, light and permanently keen, of no recorded ornament. It belongs to House Roxton of the Ring, a middling house of the Reach whose fortunes have never been great but whose blade puts them in company far above their acreage. There is a certain plainness to the whole object that suits the name.",
        "And the name is the point. Most Valyrian swords are called something grand or something mournful — the great houses like a blade that sounds like an epic. This one is called what a sword actually does to the families of the men it is used on, and declines to dress the transaction up in chivalry. As with all of them, it cannot be remade: the art was lost with the Freehold and no living smith can do more than reheat and reshape what exists, which is why a modest house that owns one never sells it, whatever the year has been like.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Ser Jon Roxton", paras: [
          "The blade’s one real appearance in the histories is during the Dance of the Dragons, in the hands of Ser Jon Roxton — called Bold Jon, a knight whose reputation for lethality was matched only by his reputation for arrogance and for a private life that made even a war-torn court raise its eyebrows. He fought for the greens, he fought well, and he was the sort of man the histories describe with a faint air of relief at not having had to share a table with him.",
          "He used Orphan-Maker as it was named to be used. That is nearly the whole of the record; the Roxtons are not a house the maesters spent much ink on before or after, and the sword vanishes back into obscurity once that war is over.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["Bold Jon Roxton met his end at Tumbleton in circumstances the chroniclers clearly enjoyed writing down — dragged from his feet in ground churned to mud, killed with his own sword still in his hand and unable to get up. Orphan-Maker is not reported anywhere afterwards with any confidence. A sword that goes missing in a sacked town during a civil war is rarely seen again, and no later Roxton is recorded carrying it."] },

    { id: "catspaw-dagger", name: "The Catspaw Dagger", sub: "Valyrian steel, dragonbone hilt",
      meta: [["Form", "Dagger"], ["Notable owners", "Petyr Baelish, Bran Stark, Arya Stark"]],
      blurb: "The smallest blade on this list, and the one that decides the most.",
      parasTitle: "The blade itself",
      paras: [
        "It is a dagger about the length of a forearm’s bone, and it is the plainest object in this collection and the most expensive-looking. The blade is Valyrian steel — dark, faintly rippled, tapering to a point fine enough to be uncomfortable to look at — and it has no ornament at all, no engraving, no inlay, nothing but the waves in the metal. The hilt is dragonbone: black, smooth, slightly warm to the touch, and worth on its own more than most knights own. There is no crossguard to speak of. The whole thing is meant to be worn under a coat and used at arm’s length in a small room.",
        "It weighs almost nothing. It goes through leather like cloth and through mail as though the rings were unlinked, and it has never needed a stone taken to it. A knife like this is an absurd thing to give a hired murderer — the equivalent of sending a man to break a window with a jewel — and everybody who ever handled it understood that, which is why the question of who owned it became so much more important than the question of who held it.",
        "The reason for that value is the same reason the whole of this list exists: Valyrian steel cannot be made. It came out of the Freehold’s forges with dragonfire and sorcery, the Doom ended both, and no smith alive can produce so much as a new nail of it. Which means that unlike a sword, which announces itself, a Valyrian dagger can be carried through a crowd unseen and still be traced afterwards by anyone who knows the short list of people wealthy enough to have owned one.",
      ],
      sections: [
        { h: "Sent to a sickroom", paras: [
          "The dagger enters the story in the hands of a hired killer, sent into a quiet room in Winterfell where a crippled boy lay senseless, with instructions to cut his throat and make it look like anything else. The attempt failed — badly, and against a direwolf — and the killer left the weapon behind him, which is the single most consequential piece of carelessness in the entire saga.",
          "Catelyn Stark took the knife south to King’s Landing to find out whose it was. That journey, and the answer she was given, put the Starks and the Lannisters at each other’s throats and lit the war. The whole plot of the first book runs through this one small object, and the extraordinary thing is that it does so entirely as evidence rather than as a weapon.",
        ] },
        { h: "Whose was it?", paras: [
          "The answer she received was a lie, and a very good one: she was told the dagger had been won at a tourney wager by a certain Lannister, in a bet on a Kingsguard knight’s joust, and the man who told her that had been the one holding the losing side of the story. The accusation was aimed to detonate, and it did.",
          "Working out the truth is harder and the story is deliberately unhelpful about it. One account, offered later and with some plausibility, holds the knife belonged to the king himself and was taken out of his armoury by a son who wanted to please him and had no sense of proportion — a boy hearing a drunk father wish aloud that an injured child would just get on with dying, and deciding to arrange it. The other reading points at the man who told the lie in the first place, on the grounds that a liar who names a false owner is usually protecting the real one, and that the war that followed was extremely convenient for him.",
          "Both explanations have supporters. The books have not settled it, and the two tellings of the story do not agree: on the screen the dagger is unambiguously the property of the man who lied about it, and he hands it on himself, years later, as a gift with a great deal of calculation behind it.",
        ] },
        { h: "Passed along", paras: [
          "After King’s Landing the knife travelled. It changed hands more times than any other blade in this collection, mostly in circumstances nobody recorded properly — pulled from a body, taken as loot, carried in a boot, given away in a courtyard to a boy in a chair who had every reason to know what it was and said nothing about it. It went north again in the end, back to the castle it had been used in, which is not the sort of thing objects usually do.",
          "That is what makes the dagger the strangest item here. Every other blade in this collection is an heirloom: it belongs to a house, it has a name, it passes from father to son and its history is a list of surnames. The catspaw dagger has no house and no proper name — it is called after the hired man who dropped it. It has spent the entire story being carried by people who acquired it by accident, and it has arguably decided more than all the great swords put together.",
        ] },
      ],
      fate: ["The same dagger, years later and several owners on, is the thing Arya Stark drives into the Night King in the godswood at Winterfell. A weapon sent to murder a Stark child ends up in a Stark child's hand ending the war it was never meant to be part of.",
        "It is also, by then, the only blade in this collection to have been used for the purpose the whole material apparently exists to serve. The great houses spent three centuries hanging their Valyrian steel on walls. The thing that finished it was a knife that had been passed hand to hand like a stolen purse."] },
  ],
}
);
